            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and  around the window frames. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was observed along the basebaords around the home, indicating there is air infiltrating into the floor, which may be part of the reason these feel cool. A large area for air infiltration was found within the crawl space around the waste water pipe access. The poly is cut open from previous work being done, and this is acting as a fairly large open hole in the floor. 

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 100 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ. I suspect an inprovement of 20% is quite achievable for your home. </airSeal>
                <ceilings>

The original attic of your house was assumed to have a total of R32 insulation. I have modelled adding 5.5 inches (R19) of blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R51.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R51, you could potentially save $ 120 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 125 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older wood framed double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all the windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 420 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 12.6 GJ.  </windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

DOMESTIC HOT WATER USING EXISTING OIL-BOILER

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly.

As you currently have a very efficient oil boiler, it would be ideal to use your boiler to heat your hot water via a dedicated loop, and incorporate an insulated storage tank (in this case your electric tank)  to prevent the boiler from "short cycling".



Savings:

By using your boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $ 770 per year in energy costs.  

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

PROPANE BOILER UPGRADE

Your current oil-fired boiler was originally rated at 81.8% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Please note, the upgrade savings below also incorporates using the boiler to heat your domestic hot water. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, and using this boiler to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $ 2,060 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOODSTOVE UPGRADE

If you decide to replace your current woodstove, I recommend you install a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. By installing a deticated combustion air supply pipe, this will add savings of $80 per year from air infilration. 



Please note, these savings below are using the current oil-fired boiler as the 'base' comparison. 



Savings:

@ $100 per cord - and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 2,200 per year in energy savings. 

@ $341.25 per cord - and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,200 per year in energy savings. 



A new woodstove would qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $ 1,000, from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

On April 1, 2019, the AEA launched the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (Program), providing up to $20,000 to help owners of older, less efficient homes achieve significant gains in the energy efficiency and home comfort. Completing an exterior wall insulation upgrade is a required first step to participate in this Program, this must be a minimum increase from the current insulation value by R10. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, there are other rebates available for reducing air leakage, upgrading windows and heating systems, and a bonus for reducing total energy use by a minimum 20%.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



If you are interested in adding addition main wall exterior insulation and accessing the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, please do not hesitate to contact me. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



